    },
    {
      "id": "2tipr3f",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Meaning of Chemical Reaction"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Chemical reactions transform reactants into products by breaking and making bonds.",
        "A balanced chemical equation strictly obeys the Law of Conservation of Mass.",
        "Key indicators of chemical changes include gas evolution, color change, temperature change, and precipitate formation.",
        "Oxidation is the gain of oxygen or loss of electrons, while reduction is the loss of oxygen or gain of electrons.",
        "Corrosion damages metals via ambient oxidation, whereas rancidity causes foul odor and taste in oxidized fats and oils."
      ],
      "id": "nt0l5cs"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "71415er",
      "content": "• A chemical reaction is a process in which one or more substances are transformed into new substances."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "rbhvzic",
      "content": "• The substances that take part in a reaction are called reactants."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ydrvan7",
      "content": "• The substances formed in a reaction are called products."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "c3my9bl",
      "content": "• Chemical reactions involve breaking and forming of chemical bonds."
    },
    {
      "id": "iw6ooat",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Chemical Equation"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "23s65b2",
      "content": "A chemical equation is a symbolic representation of a chemical reaction using chemical formulas."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "dyjz6la",
      "content": "Example:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "cqzv65i",
      "content": "2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O"
    },
    {
      "id": "grqkd1l",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Balanced Chemical Equation"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "w5w7vva",
      "content": "A balanced chemical equation has an equal number of atoms of each element on both sides."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "k2y1z1l",
      "content": "Example:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "h4v5i6g",
      "content": "CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O"
    },
    {
      "id": "aawv4ew",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Indicators of Chemical Reaction"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "zgtiiur",
      "content": "• Change in colour"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "c8vkf35",
      "content": "• Change in temperature"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "o2xj7v5",
      "content": "• Formation of gas"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "yauj0w1",
      "content": "• Formation of precipitate"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "m6cjx8g",
      "content": "• Emission of light or sound"
    },
    {
      "id": "z9o9u25",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5. Types of Chemical Reactions"
    },
    {
      "id": "pnwg983",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Reaction Type</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>General Form</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Example</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Combination Reaction</p>",
            "<p>A + B → AB</p>",
            "<p>2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Decomposition Reaction</p>",
            "<p>AB → A + B</p>",
            "<p>CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Displacement Reaction</p>",
            "<p>A + BC → AC + B</p>",
            "<p>Zn + CuSO₄ → ZnSO₄ + Cu</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Double Displacement Reaction</p>",
            "<p>AB + CD → AD + CB</p>",
            "<p>AgNO₃ + NaCl → AgCl + NaNO₃</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c589qcv",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "6. Oxidation and Reduction"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "r5d7vd6",
      "content": "Oxidation: Addition of oxygen or loss of hydrogen."
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Parameter",
        "Oxidation",
        "Reduction"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Oxygen",
          "Gain of Oxygen",
          "Loss of Oxygen"
        ],
        [
          "Hydrogen",
          "Loss of Hydrogen",
          "Gain of Hydrogen"
        ],
        [
          "Electrons",
          "Loss of Electrons",
          "Gain of Electrons"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "w5c6btq"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "qzcc2jr",
      "content": "Reduction: Removal of oxygen or gain of hydrogen."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ab6jeri",
      "content": "These reactions together are called redox reactions."
    },
    {
      "id": "hfbyp74",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "7. Exothermic and Endothermic Reactions"
    },
    {
      "id": "aii8615",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Reaction Type</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Explanation</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Exothermic Reaction</p>",
            "<p>Releases heat energy</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Endothermic Reaction</p>",
            "<p>Absorbs heat energy</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "esmjmgd",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "8. Corrosion"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "lzmdook",
      "content": "Corrosion is the gradual destruction of metals due to chemical reactions with the environment. Rusting of iron is the most common example."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "79kx463",
      "content": "Example of rusting:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "0k6gmq8",
      "content": "Iron + Oxygen + Water → Rust"
    },
    {
      "id": "6p8cfup",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "9. Rancidity"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "jpw5sod",
      "content": "Rancidity refers to the oxidation of fats and oils in food which causes unpleasant smell and taste. It can be prevented by storing food in airtight containers."
    },
    {
      "id": "wx76hwa",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "10. Catalysts"
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Respiration is classified as an exothermic reaction, while photosynthesis is endothermic.",
        "Flushing packaged chips with inert Nitrogen gas prevents rancidity by blocking oxidation.",
        "Rusting of iron requires both oxygen and moisture, forming hydrated ferric oxide (Fe2O3·xH2O).",
        "Catalysts alter the chemical reaction speed without being consumed in the process."
      ],
      "id": "bbiz2sr"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "pcmps6s",
      "content": "A catalyst is a substance that increases the rate of a chemical reaction without being consumed in the reaction."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "e9ham58",
      "content": "Examples: Platinum, Iron catalyst in Haber process"
    },
    {
      "id": "1c9nq2t",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "Important Facts"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5dos6ew",
      "content": "• Chemical equations represent chemical reactions."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "47l4tgk",
      "content": "• Rusting of iron is a common example of corrosion."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "l2q8lax",
      "content": "• Redox reactions involve oxidation and reduction."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "1fjfu7e",
      "content": "• Exothermic reactions release heat energy."
    }
  ],
  "enriched": true
}